     1	// Phase 1 game event / vibration engine. Cites vanilla
     2	// net.minecraft.world.level.gameevent.{GameEvent, GameEventListener, GameEventDispatcher,
     3	// PositionSource, BlockPositionSource, EntityPositionSource} and
     4	// .vibrations.{VibrationSystem, VibrationSelector, VibrationInfo}.
     5	//
     6	// Deliberate scope reductions vs. vanilla:
     7	// - Vanilla shards listeners per chunk-section (EuclideanGameEventListenerRegistry) for
     8	//   performance at scale. This is a single flat per-world Vec<Arc<dyn GameEventListener>>,
     9	//   registered/unregistered explicitly.
    10	// - Vanilla's VibrationSystem.Ticker simulates a per-listener travel time before a
    11	//   selected vibration is actually delivered, and streams a travelling particle to
    12	//   clients. This resolves synchronously in the same tick the event is emitted; the
    13	//   VibrationSelector in vibration.rs is ported and unit-tested but not yet wired into
    14	//   this path (needs a per-tick driver, deferred to a future phase).
    15	// - Vanilla's occlusion check (VibrationSystem.Listener.isOccluded) nudges the ray in
    16	//   all 6 directions before a ClipBlockStateContext raycast. This does a straight-line
    17	//   block-center sampling approximation against the same
    18	//   minecraft:occludes_vibration_signals tag instead of a full raycast.
