     1	//! Port of `net.minecraft.world.entity.ai.Brain` / `ActivityData` /
     2	// Legacy invariant checks retained for vanilla behavior; migrate these paths before removing this allow.
     3	#![allow(clippy::unwrap_used, clippy::expect_used, clippy::panic)]
     4	//! `net.minecraft.world.entity.schedule.Activity` (26.2 decompile).
     5	//!
     6	//! # Why this is not the Goal system
     7	//!
     8	//! Modern vanilla AI (villager, piglin, allay, warden, frog, camel, ...) is Brain-driven, not
     9	//! Goal-driven. The two arbitrate completely differently and MUST NOT be conflated:
    10	//!
    11	//! - `GoalSelector` (`crate::entity::ai::goal::goal_selector`) gives goals mutual exclusion by
    12	//!   control bit (`goals_by_control: [usize; 4]`): at most one goal owns MOVE at a time.
    13	//! - `Brain` has no such thing. `Brain.startEachNonRunningBehavior` (`Brain.java:409-424`)
    14	//!   walks the whole priority `TreeMap` and calls `tryStart` on *every* stopped behavior in
    15	//!   *every* active activity whose memory conditions hold. Several behaviors run concurrently
    16	//!   in the same tick. Contention is resolved by **memory ownership** instead: many behaviors
    17	//!   write `WALK_TARGET`, and exactly one terminal sink (`MoveToTargetSink`) reads it and
    18	//!   drains it into the navigator. The priority map only orders the `tryStart` attempts within
    19	//!   a tick; it is not exclusivity.
    20	//!
    21	//! Do not "fix" the behavior loop below by adding `Controls`-style slots. That would silently
    22	//! change arbitration semantics away from vanilla.
    23	//!
    24	//! # Concurrency
    25	//!
    26	//! A `Brain` is deliberately split into two independently locked halves:
    27	//!
    28	//! - [`MemoryStore`] stays live behind its own short-held mutex. Memories are written from
    29	//!   outside the owning mob's AI tick -- `LivingEntity::damage_with_context` writes `HURT_BY`,
    30	//!   game-event listeners write note-block memories -- so taking the memory map out of its
    31	//!   mutex with `mem::take` for the duration of the tick would drop those writes onto a
    32	//!   throwaway `Default` and lose them silently.
    33	//! - [`BrainRuntime`] (sensors, behavior tables, active activities) IS taken out with
    34	//!   `std::mem::take` for the tick, exactly like `GoalSelector` in `Mob::tick`
    35	//!   (`entity/mob/mod.rs:967-985`), because it genuinely has a single owner during a tick.
    36	//!
    37	//! Lock order, leaf-last: `runtime` -> `memory`, and `memory` is never held while taking the
    38	//! mob's `navigator` / `look_control` / `move_control` locks. The runtime take is guarded so
    39	//! an interrupted sensor await restores the live runtime instead of losing it.
    40	
    41	pub mod behavior;
    42	pub mod memory;
    43	pub mod sensor;
